1/8002`, keeps them alive under SIGKILL (HTTP respawn ≤60s; Redis respawn after kill), and exposes a stable `GET /api/health` contract returning `{status:\"ok\", node_id}` with `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` matching ports. This is the foundation every later auth, messaging, WS, IRC, and SPA task assumes: process topology, port map, node identity, and local-only Redis.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n- **Process model:** One bash supervisor (`/app/start.sh`) stays in the foreground and owns child lifetimes. Children: one `redis-server` + three Node.js HTTP workers. The supervisor polls PIDs (≈1s cadence) and respawns any dead child without exiting.\n- **HTTP runtime:** **Node.js** (v24 present in the environment) using the **stdlib `http` module only** for this task—no npm registry dependency for bootstrap. One shared entrypoint, differentiated solely by `NODE_ID` / `PORT` env vars.\n- **Redis:** System `redis-server` (v7 present), bound to `127.0.0.1:6379`, persistence directory under `/app/data/redis`. Started as a non-daemon child of `start.sh` (no `--daemonize yes`) so the supervisor can track its PID.\n- **Bind address:** HTTP listens on `127.0.0.1` (epic: nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`). Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379`. No Docker, no remote broker, no cloud services.\n\n### Alternatives con\n====\nstatus closed\ndepends None\nAddressed FEEDBACK attempt=1: rebased auth onto MARATHON-2 head so start.sh, redis.conf, and bootstrap tests remain; DDL in server/db.js; register/login/me + cross-node SQLite verified; Redis-down auth verified.\n","stderr":"","executionTime":121,"interleavedOutput":"status closed\nImplemented start.sh supervisor (Redis :6379 + nodes :8000/8001/8002 with ~1s respawn), server/index.js + health.js (GET /api/health → {status:ok, node_id} number, Redis-independent), package.json, redis.conf. Verified: unit tests, integration (health map, node SIGKILL respawn ≤60s, Redis SIGKILL with health still 200). No migration.\n---\n## Summary\n\nMARATHO